{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1533030-boons-based-on-wordplay","id":1533030,"title":"Boons Based on Wordplay","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-03-22T21:04:11-07:00","id":1533030,"title":"Boons Based on Wordplay","content":"Some of the Boons are based on a wordplay of the Purview and not based on the concept of the Perview. But does these Boons it make any sense, if not used in context with modern day English?<br /> <br /> Example; the Frost Perview has \u201cCooler Heads\u201d. This Boon will calm down anyone with strong emotions, making them emotionless and make them leave the situation.<br /> In the text, it is based on wordplays like \u201cCold-hearted\u201d, \u201cflaring tempers\u201d and \u201ccooling things down\u201d, cold being the opposite of hot.<br /> But why would frost make people emotionless and leave?<br /> <br /> In Danish \u201cCool Headed\u201c means being super focused, not necessarily calm or emotionless. And the direct translation of \u201cflaring tempers\u201d would be something like \u201chigh seas\u201d.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3356-kalle","name":"Kalle","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"30"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-24T15:19:23-07:00","id":1533093,"title":"","content":"imho it's as symbolic as it's wordplay. like, a lot of boons work by relying on the cultural perceptions and the symbolisms humans have given to such symbols. it's even written in the purview description. usually these symbolisms go beyond just a particular culture, especially in the ages of globalization, so there are chances that non-english speakers would still get the general gist of the boon/the symbolism at play.<br /> <br /> as for your example, well, you explained it yourself. cold being the opposite of hot. cold rationality versus fiery temper. the boon doesn't just make people emotionless, they make irrational people rational enough to think &quot;hey, this might be a stupid decision, perhaps i should choose another option.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27963-jayng2183","name":"JayNg2183","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27963_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-27T09:41:46-07:00","id":1533257,"title":"","content":"That's true, boons should also represent symbolic aspects of a Purview. Boons are miracles, not super powers.<br /> But symbols (and word play) are very hard to translate between languages, and cultures even more so.<br /> <br /> I guess you could say American English is as close to a modern globalized language as you can get.<br /> And I get why it would be a near impossible task to make boons with universal symbolism.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3356-kalle","name":"Kalle","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"30"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}